victoria-metrics (fast time series database)

VictoriaMetrics is a fast, scalable and reliable time series database and
monitoring solution. It is written in Go and is available as a single
self-contained binary.

This package bundles all VictoriaMetrics components in one Slackware
package:

  * victoria-metrics - the single-node server (port 8428 by default)
  * vminsert, vmselect, vmstorage - the cluster components (only on
    amd64 and arm64, where the upstream cluster tarball is published)
  * vmagent - data collection agent
  * vmalert, vmalert-tool - alerting and anomaly detection
  * vmauth - authentication and authorization proxy
  * vmbackup, vmrestore - backup and restore tools
  * vmctl - command line management utility

Groupname and Username

You must have the "victoria-metrics" group and user to run this script,
for example:

  groupadd -g 369 victoria-metrics
  useradd -u 369 -s /bin/false -d /var/lib/victoria-metrics victoria-metrics

Starting the server

The package installs /etc/rc.d/rc.victoria-metrics.new which is activated
by doinst.sh.  To start VictoriaMetrics at boot, add the following to
/etc/rc.d/rc.local:

  if [ -x /etc/rc.d/rc.victoria-metrics ]; then
    /etc/rc.d/rc.victoria-metrics start
  fi

The server stores its data in /var/lib/victoria-metrics and writes its
log to /var/log/victoria-metrics/victoria-metrics.log.  Additional
command line options can be set in /etc/default/victoria-metrics.

vmagent persistent queue

vmagent buffers received metrics in an on-disk queue before forwarding
them to the storage.  The queue is stored in
/var/lib/victoria-metrics/vmagent-queue (set via -remoteWrite.tmpDataPath
in /etc/default/vmagent).  The rc script creates this directory and
gives it to the "victoria-metrics" user automatically.

If the queue directory is not writable by the service user, vmagent
fails to start with "permission denied" on the queue path.  This can
happen if vmagent is started from a directory the user cannot write to
(e.g. /root), because without -remoteWrite.tmpDataPath vmagent creates
the queue in the current working directory.

Storing data on a dedicated partition

If you have a dedicated data partition (e.g. mounted at /data), point
VictoriaMetrics to a directory on it.  Edit /etc/default/victoria-metrics
and set:

  DATADIR=/data/victoria-metrics

Then create the directory and give it to the service user:

  mkdir -p /data/victoria-metrics
  chown victoria-metrics:victoria-metrics /data/victoria-metrics
  chmod 0750 /data/victoria-metrics

The rc script creates the directory automatically if it does not exist
and fixes ownership, so the chown/chmod above are only needed if you
want to set them explicitly.  Restart the server afterwards:

  /etc/rc.d/rc.victoria-metrics restart

Documentation: https://docs.victoriametrics.com/


Setup with nginx + vmauth + vmagent
===================================

The recommended production setup is:

  client -> nginx (TLS) -> vmauth (token check) -> vmagent -> victoria-metrics

All VictoriaMetrics components listen only on 127.0.0.1.  nginx terminates
TLS and proxies to vmauth, which checks the Bearer token and proxies to
vmagent, which forwards the metrics to the storage.

Files installed by the package:

  /etc/victoria-metrics/vmauth.conf.new   - vmauth config (token check)
  /etc/default/vmagent.new                - vmagent options
  /etc/default/vmauth.new                 - vmauth options
  /etc/rc.d/rc.vmagent.new                - vmagent init script
  /etc/rc.d/rc.vmauth.new                 - vmauth init script
  /etc/nginx/conf.d/victoria-metrics.conf.new - nginx server block example

All .new files are activated by doinst.sh on package install (or upgrade).

1. Install the package and create the user/group (see above).

2. Set a strong token in the vmauth config:

     # edit /etc/victoria-metrics/vmauth.conf and replace
     # CHANGE_ME_STRONG_TOKEN with a real secret

3. Start the components (as root):

     /etc/rc.d/rc.victoria-metrics start
     /etc/rc.d/rc.vmagent start
     /etc/rc.d/rc.vmauth start

4. Configure nginx.  The example server block is installed at
   /etc/nginx/conf.d/victoria-metrics.conf.new.  Edit it (server_name,
   TLS certificate paths) and rename it to victoria-metrics.conf:

     mv /etc/nginx/conf.d/victoria-metrics.conf.new \
        /etc/nginx/conf.d/victoria-metrics.conf
     nginx -t && /etc/rc.d/rc.nginx reload

5. Test the whole chain.  Push a test metric with the token.

   Prometheus text format via /api/v1/import/prometheus (plain text,
   no compression - this is what most clients use):

     curl -sS -X POST \
       -H "Authorization: Bearer CHANGE_ME_STRONG_TOKEN" \
       --data-binary "test_metric 42 $(($(date +%s) * 1000))" \
       https://metrics.example.com/api/v1/import/prometheus

   With labels:

     curl -sS -X POST \
       -H "Authorization: Bearer CHANGE_ME_STRONG_TOKEN" \
       --data-binary "cpu_load{host=\"web1\",dc=\"eu\"} 0.75 $(($(date +%s) * 1000))" \
       https://metrics.example.com/api/v1/import/prometheus

   Or VictoriaMetrics JSON format via /api/v1/import:

     curl -sS -X POST \
       -H "Authorization: Bearer CHANGE_ME_STRONG_TOKEN" \
       --data-binary "{\"metric\":{\"__name__\":\"test_metric\"},\"timestamps\":[$(($(date +%s) * 1000))],\"values\":[42]}" \
       https://metrics.example.com/api/v1/import

   Read the data back (read access must be configured in vmauth.conf):

     curl -sS -H "Authorization: Bearer CHANGE_ME_STRONG_TOKEN" \
       'https://metrics.example.com/api/v1/export?match=test_metric'

   Notes:
   - The timestamp must be in milliseconds and not older than the
     retention period (default 1 year).
   - The Prometheus remote write endpoint /api/v1/write expects
     snappy-compressed bodies (Prometheus standard).  Use
     /api/v1/import/prometheus for plain-text Prometheus format.
   - For metrics with labels, use match={__name__=~"..."} or
     match[]=... in the export query.


Sending metrics without a timestamp
===================================

Clients without a reliable time source (e.g. embedded devices) can
send metrics WITHOUT a timestamp - VictoriaMetrics will use the server
time when the data is received.

Prometheus text format without timestamp:

  temp 25.5
  power{device="sensor-1"} 3.3

Example curl:

  curl -sS -X POST \
    -H "Authorization: Bearer CHANGE_ME_STRONG_TOKEN" \
    --data-binary 'temp 25.5' \
    https://metrics.example.com/api/v1/import/prometheus

  curl -sS -X POST \
    -H "Authorization: Bearer CHANGE_ME_STRONG_TOKEN" \
    --data-binary 'power{device="sensor-1"} 3.3' \
    https://metrics.example.com/api/v1/import/prometheus

Multiple metrics in one request (no timestamps)
-----------------------------------------------

Several Prometheus text lines can be sent in a single request, one
per line.  All samples get the same server receive time.

  curl -sS -X POST \
    -H "Authorization: Bearer CHANGE_ME_STRONG_TOKEN" \
    --data-binary $'temp 25.5\nhumidity 60.1\npower{device="sensor-1"} 3.3' \
    https://metrics.example.com/api/v1/import/prometheus

The body is just the lines joined with newlines:

  temp 25.5
  humidity 60.1
  power{device="sensor-1"} 3.3

IMPORTANT:
- The Prometheus text format via /api/v1/import/prometheus accepts
  lines WITHOUT a timestamp.  The server time is used in this case.
- The VictoriaMetrics JSON format via /api/v1/import REQUIRES the
  "timestamps" array - it cannot be omitted.
- The Prometheus remote write endpoint /api/v1/write (snappy) also
  accepts samples without a timestamp - the server time is used.
- The simplest client is a plain HTTP POST with the Prometheus text
  body.  No snappy compression is needed.
- Note: without a timestamp, all samples get the server receive time.
  If several samples are sent in one request, they will all have the
  same timestamp.


Grafana setup
=============

Grafana has a native "VictoriaMetrics" data source type (in addition
to the generic Prometheus type).  Both work with this setup.  The
requests go through the same chain as everything else:

  Grafana -> nginx (TLS) -> vmauth (token check) -> victoria-metrics

1. Add a read-only user for Grafana in /etc/victoria-metrics/vmauth.conf.
   Uncomment the read user section and set a separate token:

     users:
     - bearer_token: "CHANGE_ME_STRONG_TOKEN"   # write token (sensors)
       url_prefix: "http://127.0.0.1:8080/"
     - bearer_token: "CHANGE_ME_READ_TOKEN"     # read token (Grafana)
       url_prefix: "http://127.0.0.1:8428/"

   Restart vmauth after editing:

     /etc/rc.d/rc.vmauth restart

2. In Grafana, add a new data source.  Two options work:

   Option A - native "VictoriaMetrics" type (recommended):

   - Type: VictoriaMetrics
   - Name: VictoriaMetrics
   - URL: https://metrics.example.com
     (the subdomain that nginx proxies to vmauth)
   - Access: "Using credentials" -> "Header" -> "Authorization"
   - Header value: Bearer CHANGE_ME_READ_TOKEN
   - Click "Save & test".  Grafana should report success.

   Option B - generic "Prometheus" type:

   - Type: Prometheus
   - Name: VictoriaMetrics
   - URL: https://metrics.example.com
   - Access: "Using credentials" -> "Header" -> "Authorization"
   - Header value: Bearer CHANGE_ME_READ_TOKEN
   - Click "Save & test".

   Both types work because VictoriaMetrics serves the Prometheus API
   paths (/prometheus/*) on the same port as the main API.

3. Create a dashboard.  In the query editor, select the metric name
   (e.g. temp) and the time range.

Notes:
- The read token only allows queries (it proxies directly to the
  single-node server, not through vmagent).  Sensors use the write
  token, which goes through vmagent.
- If you want Grafana to also write (e.g. for annotations), give it
  the write token instead, but then it can also modify data.
- The vmauth config in v1.152.0 has no "read_only" field - access
  control is done by routing: the read user points to the server
  directly, the write user points to vmagent.
- For testing without vmauth, Grafana can also connect directly to
  the victoria-metrics port (127.0.0.1:8428) without authentication,
  but this bypasses the token check and should only be used for
  local testing.

Ports used (all bound to 127.0.0.1 only):

  * 8428  - victoria-metrics (single-node server)
  * 8080  - vmagent (receives metrics from vmauth)
  * 8081  - vmauth (receives requests from nginx)

To start all components at boot, add to /etc/rc.d/rc.local:

  for svc in victoria-metrics vmagent vmauth; do
    if [ -x /etc/rc.d/rc.$svc ]; then
      /etc/rc.d/rc.$svc start
    fi
  done


Gotchas
=======

- vmagent: without -remoteWrite.tmpDataPath it creates its queue in the
  current working directory, which a service user can't write to.  Point
  it at a writable dir (see /etc/default/vmagent).
- vmagent: -remoteWrite.url must include the path (/api/v1/write),
  otherwise it posts to / and gets 400.
- /api/v1/write expects snappy-compressed bodies (Prometheus standard).
  For plain text use /api/v1/import/prometheus.
- Timestamps are in milliseconds; samples older than the retention
  period (default 1 year) are rejected.
- vmauth v1.152.0 config uses a users: list (not write_user/read_user),
  and has no read_only field - restrict access by routing: read user ->
  server directly, write user -> vmagent.
